<HowtoLayout slug="ai-white-background-packshot" ctaLabel="Create your account" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">An AI product photo on a white background can pass Amazon, Google and Shopify with one file. Start from a photo of the real product, not from a prompt, and build to the narrow band where the three rules overlap.</p>

  <h2>How do you make an AI product photo with a white background that passes?</h2>

  <p>Edit a real photo of the product rather than generating one from words. In hubStudio, upload it to the Edit an image job, ask for a pure white background, render at 4K, then crop it square in the Image editor and save a 2048-pixel JPEG. Check the white, the fill and the metadata before upload.</p>

  <p>The main image is the one picture every listing has to get right. Amazon says every product needs at least one compliant main image, and without one it "may temporarily remove the product listing from search." Google says an image that misses its requirements "won't appear on Google surfaces." Extra angles and lifestyle shots are optional on both. The main image isn't.</p>

  <h2>What do Amazon, Google and Shopify require of the main image?</h2>

  <p>Each platform publishes its own rules, and none of the three pages carries a date. So the date that counts is the day they were read: October 8, 2026, for Amazon's US store, Google Merchant Center and Shopify's help center.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Rule</th>
          <th scope="col">Amazon (MAIN image)</th>
          <th scope="col">Google Merchant Center (image_link)</th>
          <th scope="col">Shopify</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Background</th>
          <td>Pure white, RGB 255, 255, 255</td>
          <td>No color set on the page</td>
          <td>No rule; your theme decides</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Product fill</th>
          <td>85 percent of the image</td>
          <td>75 to 90 percent (best practice)</td>
          <td>No rule</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Minimum size</th>
          <td>500 px on the longest side; 1,000 or more turns on zoom</td>
          <td>500 x 500 px, for all products from January 31, 2027; 1500 x 1500 recommended</td>
          <td>None stated; 2048 x 2048 recommended for square</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Maximum</th>
          <td>10,000 px on the longest side</td>
          <td>64 megapixels and 16 MB</td>
          <td>5000 x 5000 px or 25 megapixels, under 20 MB</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Formats</th>
          <td>JPEG (recommended), TIFF, PNG, non-animated GIF</td>
          <td>JPEG and WebP recommended; PNG, GIF, BMP, TIFF</td>
          <td>PNG best, then JPEG; WebP, TIFF and others accepted</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Banned</th>
          <td>Props not included, packaging unless it's a feature, any part of a mannequin, more than one unit, the product shown twice, placeholders, reviews, claims, Amazon logos and badges</td>
          <td>Promotional text, price, watermarks, added logos, barcodes, borders, placeholders, mockups, products not sold together</td>
          <td>Nothing stated on the media page</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">AI disclosure</th>
          <td>Only for photorealistic people made entirely by AI</td>
          <td>Metadata on every image made with generative AI</td>
          <td>Nothing stated</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Amazon's main image needs "a pure white background (RGB color values: 255, 255, 255)" and the product shown "as 85% of the image," whole, once and as a single unit. Read from Amazon's own rule text for the US store.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central, Product image guide, US store, page undated, read October 8, 2026. https://sellercentral.amazon.com/help/hub/reference/external/G1881</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google asks for a product that "takes up no less than 75%, but not more than 90%, of the full image," at 500 by 500 pixels or more, with 1500 by 1500 recommended, and no file over 64 megapixels or 16MB. Read from Google's own help page for the main product image.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Merchant Center Help, Image link [image_link], page undated, read October 8, 2026. https://support.google.com/merchants/answer/6324350</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Shopify takes product images up to 5000 by 5000 pixels or 25 megapixels, under 20 MB, and says "a size of 2048 x 2048 px usually displays best" for square product images. Read from Shopify's own help center.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shopify Help Center, Product media types, page undated, read October 8, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/product-media/product-media-types</p>

  <p>Shopify sets almost nothing on its own. Its Google &amp; YouTube channel, though, "automatically syncs your products" to Google Merchant Center, so a store that sells on Google inherits Google's rules for the same image.</p>

  <h2>Where do the three rules pull apart?</h2>

  <p>In four places, and each one changes how you make the file.</p>

  <p><strong>Background.</strong> Google's Merchant Center page for the main image sets no background color at all. The line about "a solid white or transparent background" sits on Google's Manufacturer Center page, as a best practice for brands that submit product data. White is still the safe choice everywhere. It's Amazon's rule, though, not Google's.</p>

  <p><strong>Fill.</strong> Amazon asks for 85 percent. Google wants 75 to 90 percent and warns that it may test subtle crops of its own. Neither page says whether the percentage is measured on the width, the height or the area. An 85 percent frame sits inside both; a product cropped edge to edge falls outside Google's window, and a small one floating in white misses both.</p>

  <p><strong>Size floor.</strong> It moves on a known date.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google enforces a 500 by 500 pixel minimum for every product from January 31, 2027. Until then the floor is 100 by 100 for most products and 250 by 250 for clothing, and images under 500 by 500 draw warnings. Read from Google's own help page on small images.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Merchant Center Help, How to fix: Image too small, page undated, read October 8, 2026. https://support.google.com/merchants/answer/12159030</p>

  <p><strong>AI disclosure.</strong> This rule lives in the file, not in the picture, so no amount of looking at the image will tell you whether it passes.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"All images created using generative AI must contain meta data indicating that the image was AI-generated," for example the IPTC DigitalSourceType tag, and that tag must not be removed. One of the listed values covers "a composite that includes synthetic elements." Read from Google's own help page.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Merchant Center Help, Image link [image_link], page undated, read October 8, 2026. https://support.google.com/merchants/answer/6324350</p>

  <p>That composite value is the closest fit for a real product photo on an AI-made background. Amazon's rule is narrower: it asks for the keyword contains-synthetic-performer only when an image shows photorealistic people generated entirely by AI. A packshot with no people needs no Amazon tag.</p>

  <h2>What file clears all three marketplaces?</h2>

  <p>Read the three pages together and one master file falls out of the overlap. It's our reading of the rules, not a spec any of the platforms publishes.</p>

