  <p>Amazon adds one warning that matters for AI work: "Do not artificially enlarge small images." Render at the size you need instead of stretching a small file afterward. The <a href="/solutions/platforms/amazon">Amazon platform page</a> and the <a href="/solutions/platforms/shopify">Shopify platform page</a> cover the rest of each listing, from the gallery to A+ and store imagery.</p>

  <h2>How do you make the packshot in the hubStudio app?</h2>

  <p><a href="/app/create">The app's create page</a> walks through the image studio and the engines. For this job the route runs from a real photo to a saved JPEG. The Image editor is free; the renders, and the Improve with AI rewrite if you use it, are charged to your prepaid balance at the price shown first.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Photograph the real unit.</strong> One clear, evenly lit photo of the exact product and variant you sell, whole, from the front. This is what keeps the result honest. Amazon wants "the real scale, quantity, and color" and Google wants "the exact item being sold." A product generated from words alone is a mockup of something nobody shipped, and Google lists mockups as grounds for disapproval.</li>
    <li><strong>Add the skill.</strong> In Skills, open the Catalog, read E-commerce packshot with View details, then click Add to my skills. From then on it shapes every Improve with AI rewrite of your image prompts.</li>
    <li><strong>Open the image studio on Edit an image.</strong> Pick a ChatGPT Image engine: they are the ones with the Background setting. Drop the photo in the box. PNG, JPEG or WebP all work, and the app resizes the upload to 1,536 pixels on the long side before it goes out.</li>
    <li><strong>Write the change.</strong> Paste the prompt below or write your own, and click Improve with AI if you want it tuned to the engine.</li>
    <li><strong>Set the options.</strong> Shape to Square, Resolution to 4K, and Background to Opaque for a white render. Pick Transparent with PNG instead if you want a cutout you can reuse on other scenes, and render it on ChatGPT Image 2.5 Flare or Sunburst: OpenAI still lists transparency on ChatGPT Image 2 as a preview. Ask for three or four images in one run. The price shows next to the button before anything renders, and a failed run isn't charged.</li>
    <li><strong>Crop and save.</strong> Click Edit on the best result to open the Image editor. In Crop, pick Square and drag the box until the product spans about 85 percent. In Save, pick JPG, keep the quality at the top, and use the 2048 px quick pick. A transparent PNG saved as JPG turns white where it was transparent.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the record.</strong> Every render stays in History with its prompt and engine. If someone else signs off listings, send the final to them in Validation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If you'd rather hand over the catalog, our studio shoots and builds marketplace sets through the <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service</a>. For prompt craft beyond this one job, the other <a href="/resources/how-to">How-to guides</a> go engine by engine.</p>

  <h2>What prompt keeps the AI inside the rules?</h2>

  <p>Start with what must not change. The first sentence protects the product; the rest describes the frame the three rules leave you.</p>

  <div class="prompt">
    <p class="prompt__label">Prompt example</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">Keep the product exactly as it is in the photo: shape, color, label, printed text, proportions and every detail. Replace the background with pure white, RGB 255, 255, 255, edge to edge, with no gradient, no floor line, no vignette and no cast shadow. Front view, centered, the whole product in frame, one unit, nothing else in the picture. Even, soft studio light from the front left. No props, no added reflections, no added text, no logo, no border. Square frame, with the product filling about 85 percent of it.</p>
  </div>

  <p>The label printed on the product is fine to keep. Google's ban on logos covers ones "added in post-production," and it allows them when they're a physical part of the product. What you can't do is let the engine redraw that label into something close but wrong.</p>

