  <p>For the metadata line, drop the final file on the Image anonymizer: it lists what the file carries, including the tags AI engines write. Read the list and close the page without downloading the clean copy. For a file bound for Google, skip Download clean copy in the image studio too, since it removes those markers. If a re-save has already stripped the tag, add it back with an IPTC-compatible metadata editor before upload.</p>

  <h2>What about Tmall and JD?</h2>

  <p>China's two big marketplaces treat the white-background image as a deliverable with rules of its own, and both keep the rule text behind a seller login. The Tmall white-background image rules insight counts what the published sources agree on, and the JD image requirements piece sets JD beside Tmall. Confirm any number there in your own seller backend before you build a batch.</p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>

  <h3>Can I use an AI-generated main image on Amazon?</h3>

  <p>Amazon's product image guide doesn't ban AI. It asks for "a realistic, professional-quality image" that shows the real scale, quantity and color of the product. An AI edit of a real photo can meet that. A product invented from a prompt can't, because it isn't the product you ship, and Amazon also bars placeholders.</p>

  <h3>Does Google Shopping require a white background?</h3>

  <p>Not on the Merchant Center page for the main image, read October 8, 2026. That page bans promotional overlays, borders, watermarks and placeholders, and asks for 75 to 90 percent fill. The white or transparent background line sits on Google's Manufacturer Center page as a best practice. White remains the safe choice.</p>

  <h3>What size should a white-background product photo be?</h3>

  <p>2048 by 2048 pixels covers all three. It clears Amazon's 1,000-pixel zoom line, Google's 1500 by 1500 recommendation and Shopify's suggestion for square images, and it sits far under every maximum. Google's 500 by 500 floor applies to all products from January 31, 2027.</p>

  <h3>How much of the frame should the product fill?</h3>

  <p>About 85 percent. That's Amazon's figure, and it sits inside Google's 75 to 90 percent window. Neither platform says whether it measures the width, the height or the area, so pick one method and use it across the catalog. Shopify sets no fill rule.</p>

  <h3>Do I have to label an AI product photo?</h3>

  <p>For Google, yes: every image made with generative AI must carry metadata saying so, and the tag must not be stripped. For Amazon, only when the image shows photorealistic people generated entirely by AI, tagged contains-synthetic-performer. A packshot with no people needs no Amazon tag. Shopify's media page sets no rule.</p>

  <h3>Can I turn a transparent PNG into a white-background image?</h3>

  <p>Yes. Render with a transparent background on ChatGPT Image 2.5 Flare or Sunburst in hubStudio, open the result in the Image editor and save it as JPG: the transparent areas turn white. Sample the corners afterward to confirm 255, 255, 255, and keep the PNG as a cutout for lifestyle scenes.</p>

  <h3>What about Tmall and JD white-background images?</h3>

  <p>They follow rules of their own that Tmall and JD keep behind a seller login. The <a href="/resources/insights/tmall-white-background-image-rules">Tmall white-background image rules insight</a> counts what published sources agree on, and the <a href="/resources/insights/jd-image-requirements-vs-tmall">JD image requirements piece</a> sets JD beside Tmall. Confirm any number in your own seller backend before you build a batch.</p>
